A re-elected Palaszczuk Government will provide funding to enable the construction of new amenities to support participation in rugby league in Ayr. 

Funding will be provided to Burdekin Rugby League to construct a 15m x 33m structure which will include the following rooms:

• 4 x player dressing rooms with showers and toilets;
• a new office; 
• referee room; 
• first aid room;
• a mens and ladies’ toilet; and
• a disabled toilet. 

This commitment is a further example of the Palaszczuk Government investing to support local construction jobs in the Burdekin as part of Queensland’s economic recovery plan. 

#Brunker4Burdekin #Labor #Queensland #Burdekin @Burdekin Roosters JRL

Have you signed up to our annual Youth Advocates Program yet? 

Don't miss out on your chance to: 

🚌 Come on a two day trip to the nation's capital Canberra, with fellow Armenian-Australian youth, as well as members of the Armenian National Committee of Australia National Board and Office;

📝 Hands-on Advocacy Workshops;

💬 A Mock Parliamentary Discussion at Parliament House on a topic of national relevance, moderated by MPs;

📑 Sessions at think tanks and media organisations;

👤 Mock Advocacy sessions with Senators and Members of Parliament;

🤝 Intimate networking opportunities with parliamentarians, think tanks and media personnel; 

❔ Attendance at Parliamentary Question Time.

Complete the following form to express your interest! https://forms.gle/og6Na97KSaZi98kA7

*You must be at least 18 years of age at the time of the program.

This is my plan for real, practical and local action on climate change - a plan that makes our climate emergency declaration more than words. 

My Practical, Local Action on Climate Change policy includes:

🌳 Double tree canopy cover across Port Phillip from 20% to 40%;
🚴‍♂️ Invest seriously in protected bike paths with much faster delivery;
♻️ Expand garden & food waste services to everyone in Port Phillip;

And much, much more.

Robbie Nyaguy - your progressive voice - Labor for Lake Ward.

Less than two weeks to go until our fabulous panel, "Rising up for our Rights", at 6.30pm on Tuesday March 23. Grab your ticket today at https://events.humanitix.com/intertwine-human-rights

Hear from four amazing speakers on how our rights intersect and overlap:

✊🏾 Marjorie Thorpe, Djab Wurrung campaign for justice; 
✊🏾 Marcella Brassett, Asylum Seeker Resource Centre;
✊🏾 Amao Leota Lu, Fa'afafine Performance Artist & Activist;
✊🏾 June Riemer, First Peoples' Disability Network. 

We'll also be exploring whether the rights framework is still useful as we head into the future of intersectional activism.

Tickets are free for First Peoples and unwaged settlers, and $10 for waged settlers.

"Actions, Not Words"  I was always so impressed with the motto from my daughters' secondary school, and am renowned in the corporate world for achieving outcomes, not just talking about them.  I wanted to summarise some of the key areas where if elected, I will drive Council to accountable and time bound actions;
Strongly challenging inappropriate Property Development;
Actioning the Bayside Climate Change Emergency Action Plan;
Re-energising Sandringham and Hampton Villages;
Improving Bayside's sports amenities and facilitating further female participation in sports;
Increased parking for Sandringham and Hampton train stations, together with a coordinated local transport plan;
Consistent and tranparent community consultation on issues affecting residents;
Accountablility of planning and spending on major projects;
Increased spending on Aged Care home visit resources;
Better community facilities, starting with the Hampton Community Centre;
Advocating for Hampton Pier, improvements for Sandringham LifeSaving Club, and protection of our foreshore.
I look forward if elected to advocating for you on these and all Council related issues with passion and energy. And yes, the below is in Latin...

